    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.strongly_connected_components -\n    EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.strongly_connected_components.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.strongly_connected_components()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get all strongly connected components as sets of node IDs.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.strongly_connected_components()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get all strongly connected components as sets of node IDs.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.strongly_connected_components()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n  .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Get all strongly connected components as sets of node IDs.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nGet all strongly connected components as sets of node IDs.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L84483-L84616){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.strongly_connected_components()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Strongly connected components](../assets/graphs/strongly-connected-components.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A directed graph: a cycle A to B to C to A, a one-way edge C to D, a two-way pair D and E, and a separate one-way edge F to G. Following arrows, only the cycle and the two-way pair are mutually reachable, so there are four strong components, A, B, C in the accent color, D, E in teal, and the singletons F in violet and G in gold, listed on the card in the same colors.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nFor directed graphs, a strongly connected component (SCC) is a maximal set of nodes where\nevery node is reachable from every other node following directed edges. Uses Kosaraju's\nalgorithm for efficient computation.\n\nFor undirected graphs, returns the same result as `weakly_connected_components()` since\nstrong connectivity equals regular connectivity when edges are bidirectional.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[set[int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   List of sets, where each set contains the node IDs in a strongly connected component.\n    Components are returned in no particular order.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nKosaraju's algorithm (1978) computes SCCs in $O(V + E)$ time with three passes:\nfirst a DFS on the original graph produces a finish-time ordering, then the edge\nset is reversed, and a second DFS on the reversed graph in reverse-finish order\ncarves out one SCC per tree it produces. The correctness argument is elegant: a\nnode's SCC is exactly the set of vertices reachable from it in both $G$ and its\ntranspose $G^\\top$. Tarjan's algorithm delivers the same guarantee in a single DFS\npass with an explicit stack; both are common choices, and any downstream code\nshould treat the returned component list as unordered.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~weakly_connected_components`) \n\n:   The components that ignore edge directions.\n\n[](`~attracting_components`) \n\n:   The components with no edges leaving them.\n\n[](`~count_strongly_connected_components`) \n\n:   The number of components alone.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\n`A`, `B` and `C` form a cycle, so each can reach the others and they make up one strongly\nconnected component. `D` is reachable from the cycle but has no way back, so it forms its\nown:\n\n::: {#d03eb804 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"C\", \"D\")], directed=True\n)\n\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\nsccs = graph.strongly_connected_components()\n[sorted(label(n) for n in c) for c in sccs]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[['A', 'B', 'C'], ['D']]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nTwo cycles joined by a single one-way edge stay separate components. An edge back the\nother way merges them into one:\n\n::: {#1bacb0dc .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nedges = [\n    (\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"),\n    (\"X\", \"Y\"), (\"Y\", \"Z\"), (\"Z\", \"X\"),\n    (\"C\", \"X\"),\n]\none_link = ec.Graph.from_edges(edges, directed=True)\ntwo_links = ec.Graph.from_edges(edges + [(\"Z\", \"A\")], directed=True)\n\n(\n    len(one_link.strongly_connected_components()),\n    len(two_links.strongly_connected_components()),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(2, 1)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Money going around in circles\n\n`payments()` is a month of payments between 18 accounts at a made-up bank, each directed\nfrom payer to payee. Its two rings of large payments, Ana, Ben and Cruz, and Finn, Gus and\nHal, turn out to be one component, along with Dee and Eve: Dee pays into the first ring\nand Ana pays Dee back, and Eve pays into the second ring and Gus pays Dee. Max and Nia,\nwho pay each other, make up the only other component of more than one account:\n\n::: {#cb299a96 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npayments = ec.payments()\ncomponents = payments.strongly_connected_components()\n\nlabel = payments.get_label_from_id\n[sorted(label(n) for n in c) for c in components if len(c) > 1]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n[['Max', 'Nia'], ['Ana', 'Ben', 'Cruz', 'Dee', 'Eve', 'Finn', 'Gus', 'Hal']]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### One payment holds the circle together\n\nGus's payment to Dee is the second ring's only way back to the rest. Here are the eight\naccounts of the large component, with the component holding Dee in the accent color. On\nthe left, the month as it was; on the right, without that payment, Finn, Gus and Hal\ndrop out and form a component of their own, in the soft color:\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#60409ac8 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nspots = {\n    \"Ana\": (-2.6, 1), \"Cruz\": (-2.6, -1), \"Ben\": (-1.4, 0),\n    \"Dee\": (0, 1), \"Eve\": (0, -1),\n    \"Gus\": (1.4, 0.8), \"Finn\": (1.4, -0.8), \"Hal\": (2.6, 0),\n}\npos = {payments.get_id_from_label(v): p for v, p in spots.items()}\ncircle = payments.subgraph(list(pos)).set_node_attrs(\"pos\", pos)\ncut = payments.subgraph(list(pos)).set_node_attrs(\"pos\", pos)\ncut.delete_edge(\"Gus\", \"Dee\")\n\ndef circles(g):\n    found = g.strongly_connected_components()\n    core = next(c for c in found if g.get_id_from_label(\"Dee\") in c)\n    rest = [n for c in found if c != core for n in c]\n    return {\"accent\": set(core), \"soft\": set(rest)}\n\ncircle.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", highlight=circles(circle), label_by=\"label\",\n    width=320, height=240, legend=False,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b